Subject: [PATCH] Keep the Start circle off the welcome footer

On a Pixel 3 the circle was drawn across "Made with love in San Francisco".
It was not a margin that was too small. The column asked for 2457px of a
2116px screen: the rows are as tall as the number of choices makes them,
so the spacer that was supposed to push the circle down collapsed to
nothing, the circle ran past the bottom padding that had been reserved for
the footer, and clipChildren="false" let it draw there. The footer was a
separate child of the frame pinned to the bottom, so it took no part in the
layout and had no say in the matter. Measured before: circle 1781-2116,
footer 1990-2039, the footer wholly inside the circle.

Two changes. The copy and the rows go inside a ScrollView that takes the
weight the spacer used to have, so when they do not fit they scroll rather
than pushing what follows off the end. And the footer joins the column
after the circle, where being laid out on top of it is no longer possible.

The reclaimed padding is spent on making all seven rows fit at rest on the
short screen: 84dp of top padding becomes 44dp, the gap under the copy 36dp
becomes 24dp, and the circle keeps a 32dp gap to the footer.

After, on a Pixel 3: circle 1583-1935, footer 2023-2072, nothing overlapping
and the circle back to its full 128dp instead of being cut off at the screen
edge. Same on a 1344x2992 emulator. At 130% and 150% font the rows overflow
into the scroll region and the circle and footer stay put, which is the point
of the change.

No behaviour is touched. The ids are the same, so buildOptions still fills
the rows, placeAtSplash still measures the circle against its parent, and
playEntry only ever set alpha on the footer.
